<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sophia Albertina of Sweden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sophia Albertina, Abbess of Quedlinburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sophia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">Albertina</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> was a Swedish princess, daughter of King Adolf Frederick and sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III</a>. She became princess-abbess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> in 1787 and was its last abbess, as the imperial abbey was secularised in 1803 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. She spent her later life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sophia-albertina-of-sweden.jpg" alt="Sophia Albertina of Sweden" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Roslin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Roslin_-_Sofia_Albertina,_Princess_of_Sweden_-_NMDrh_44_-_Nationalmuseum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sophia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">Albertina</a> was born on 8 October 1753, the daughter of King Adolf Frederick and Louisa Ulrika of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and was named after her grandmothers, Queen Sophia Dorothea of Prussia and Margravine Albertina Frederica of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a>. She lived under her mother's strict control until the queen dowager's death in 1782 and sided with her in the quarrels with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III</a>, burning some of her mother's papers before the king could see them. Marriages to Crown Prince Frederick William of Prussia, to her cousin Prince Peter of Holstein-Gottorp and to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a> were proposed, and none took place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1767 her uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> had her made coadjutrix of Quedlinburg Abbey, a convent of Lutheran women, and in 1787 she succeeded her aunt Anna Amalia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, taking her oath on 15 October. As reigning head of a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> she founded schools for poor children, opened the city's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and raised the pay of the clergy, governing with her chancellor Sebastian von Moltzer. In the German Mediatization the state was incorporated into Prussia; she kept the title and income for life and left in September 1803.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> of 1789 she watched the sessions through a secret window and, with her sister-in-law Hedvig Elisabeth Charlotte, was associated with the social boycott of the king by noblewomen opposed to the Union and Security Act. She intervened for Magdalena Rudenschöld during the Armfelt conspiracy and had her death sentence revoked. In 1795 she came to believe that her chambermaid Lolotte Forssberg was her sister, which caused a scandal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>; in 1799 she declared Forssberg her illegitimate half-sister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1807 she lost her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> income when the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> annexed it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> did not answer her appeal. In the Revolution of 1809 she greeted Georg Adlersparre from her balcony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xiv-john">Charles XIV John</a> used her presence, as the last of the old dynasty, to lend his own legitimacy; she placed the bridal crown on Josephine of Leuchtenberg in 1823. She died on 17 March 1829. Sofia Albertina Church in Landskrona, opened in 1788, bears her name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sophia Albertina, Abbess of Quedlinburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its last abbess was <b>Sophia Albertina of Sweden</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-quedlinburg">Imperial Abbey of Quedlinburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in The Royal Court Parish, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in The Royal Court Parish, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.33,18.07&amp;year=1802" aria-label="The Royal Court Parish on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 170 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sophia Albertina of Sweden"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 440h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.33,18.07&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
